# Competitor presentation parity (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-10-01), verbatim:

> **Hard Rule: Always look at how our major competitors do things
> graphically, organizationally, interactively with customers, feature
> sharing, public presentation, etc. We need to look as close as possible
> to their processes and interactions.**

## The rule

> **Before designing or revising any website page, product page, partner
> program page, pricing page, trial or demo flow, documentation layout,
> customer communication, or operator workflow, study how the major
> Competitors do the same thing. Match their structure, navigation, page
> types, graphic conventions, feature-presentation format, and customer
> interaction steps as closely as possible. Differ only where AIC has a
> capability they lack, and present that difference inside their format.**

Buyers, partners, and assessors already know the Competitors' patterns.
A site or workflow that follows those patterns reads as an established
vendor. One that invents its own reads as unfamiliar.

## Who to study

Privileged access and identity: CyberArk, Delinea, BeyondTrust, Keeper,
HashiCorp, 1Password Business, Bitwarden Enterprise, Saviynt, SailPoint.
CMMC and compliance: PreVeil, Microsoft (GCC High and Azure Government),
Exostar, Vanta, Drata, Thoropass, and CMMC-focused MSPs. Channel and
partner programs: Microsoft, then the privileged access vendors above.

## What to compare

| Area | Look at |
| --- | --- |
| Graphics | Hero layout, product screenshots versus illustrations, diagram style, logo strips, icon style, color use, page density |
| Organization | Top navigation, mega-menu groupings, Platform / Solutions / Industries / Resources / Partners / Company split, solution-by-use-case and solution-by-industry pages, URL and slug shapes |
| Customer interaction | Free trial, demo request, guided tour, contact form fields, chat, pricing visibility, "talk to sales" placement, onboarding steps |
| Feature sharing | Capability matrices, product comparison grids, datasheets, solution briefs, integration directories, technical documentation, API references, release notes, product status labels |
| Public presentation | Proof points: customer counts, analyst placements, certifications held by the vendor, platform and partner logos, trust and security center pages, compliance mappings |
| Partner programs | Tiers, margins and incentives, deal registration, not-for-resale licenses, enablement, partner portal, marketplace listings |

## Required agent behavior

1. Before drafting a public or customer-facing surface, look at how at
 least three Competitors present the same thing. Use their live public
 pages. Record the pattern found in the commit body or `docs/dev`.
2. Adopt the shared pattern. Where Competitors disagree, follow the
 majority or the market leader.
3. Follow the Competitors' process steps for customer interactions:
 trial, demo, quote, onboarding, support, partner enrollment.
4. Do not copy their text, images, screenshots, or trademarks. Match the
 format, then write original content and use original images.
5. Website and product copy still never names a Competitor
 ([`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc)).
 Write "Competitors" where a reference is needed. Vendor names stay in
 this rule, `docs/dev`, and commit bodies. The one exception is the
 sourced, dated CMMC comparison pages under `website-and-ui/compare/`,
 described in that rule.
6. Parity of format never licenses a claim. Status words, the no-Met
 rule, and the single assessment disclaimer still apply
 ([`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc)).
 A Competitor publishing a claim AIC cannot support is not a reason to
 publish it.
7. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change
 set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Forbidden

- Designing a page layout, navigation, partner program page, or
 customer flow without first looking at how Competitors do it.
- Inventing a page type, interaction step, or feature-presentation format
 when Competitors share a common one.
- Copying Competitor text, graphics, screenshots, or trademarks.
- Naming a Competitor in website, product, or customer copy.
- Adopting a Competitor's claim that AIC cannot support with evidence.

